.TH "FLOX\-BUILD" "1" "" "" "Flox User Manuals"
.SH NAME
flox\-build \- Build packages with Flox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
flox [<general\-options>] build
     [\-d=<path>]
     [\-\-stability <stability>]
     [<package>]...
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Build the specified \f[CR]<package>\f[R] from the environment in
\f[CR]<path>\f[R], and the output at \f[CR]result\-<package>\f[R]
adjacent to the environment.
.SS Manifest\-defined packages
Possible values for \f[CR]<package>\f[R] are all keys under the
\f[CR]build\f[R] attribute in the \f[CR]manifest.toml\f[R].
If no \f[CR]<package>\f[R] is specified, Flox will attempt to build all
packages that are defined in the \f[CR]manifest.toml\f[R].
.PP
Packages are built by running the script defined in
\f[CR]build.<package>.command\f[R] within a \f[CR]bash\f[R] subshell.
The shell will behave as if \f[CR]flox activate\f[R] was run immediately
prior to running the build script.
.SS Pure and impure builds
Builds can be performed in a sandbox for the sake of reproducibility.
By default this sandbox is turned off and the build is run in the root
of the repository.
This allows you to perform incremental builds using existing caches and
intermediate build artifacts.
We call this an \(lqimpure\(rq build.
With the sandbox disabled, building is similar to running the build
script manually within a shell created by \f[CR]flox activate\f[R].
.PP
Pure builds are run in a temporary directory with the sandbox enabled.
The sandbox can be enabled by setting
\f[CR]build.<package>.sandbox = \(dqpure\(dq\f[R].
Only files tracked by \f[CR]git\f[R] are copied into the temporary build
directory.
This ensures that the build doesn\(cqt accidentally depend on untracked
files.
.PP
For this kind of \(lqsandboxed\(rq build, access to untracked files and
files outside of the repository are restricted to provide a reproducible
build environment.
Sandboxed builds on Linux additionally are restricted from accessing the
network, but the sandboxing mechanisms on macOS are somewhat limited and
thus pure builds on macOS \f[I]will be able to access the network\f[R].
.SS Referring to other builds
Any build can access the \f[I]results\f[R] of other builds (including
non\-sandboxed ones) by referring to their name via
\f[CR]${<package>}\f[R].
This allows multi\-stage builds.
In the example below, the \f[CR]app\f[R] package depends on the
\f[CR]dep\f[R] package by using \f[CR]${deps}/node_modules\f[R].
.SS Build outputs
\f[CR]flox build\f[R] creates a temporary directory for the build script
to output built packages to.
The environment variable \f[CR]out\f[R] is set to this directory, and
the build script is expected to copy or move packages to
\f[CR]$out\f[R].
.PP
Upon completion of the build, the build result will be symlinked to
\f[CR]result\-<package>\f[R] adjacent to the \f[CR].flox\f[R] directory
that defines the package.
.SS Metadata
Specifying the \f[CR]build.<package>.description\f[R] and
\f[CR]build.<package>.version\f[R] fields of the build provide extra
metadata that can be used by \f[CR]flox install\f[R],
\f[CR]flox search\f[R], and \f[CR]flox show\f[R] commands if the build
is later published.
.PP
The \f[CR]build.<package>.version\f[R] field can be specified in one of
the following ways:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]as a string\f[R]: \f[CR]version = \(dq0.0.1\(dq\f[R]
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]as read from a file\f[R]: \f[CR]version.file = \(dq<path>\(dq\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]as returned by a command\f[R]:
\f[CR]version.command = \(dq<cmd> <args>\(dq\f[R]
.SS Catalog imports for Nix expression builds
Nix expression builds (packages defined as \f[CR].nix\f[R] files under
\f[CR].flox/pkgs/\f[R]) can depend on packages from external catalogs.
Declare catalog sources in \f[B]\f[CB]nix\-builds.toml(5)\f[B]\f[R],
then run \f[CR]flox build update\-catalogs\f[R] to resolve and lock
them.
The resulting lockfile pins every catalog to a specific revision,
ensuring reproducible builds.
.PP
See \f[B]\f[CB]flox\-build\-update\-catalogs(1)\f[B]\f[R] and
\f[B]\f[CB]nix\-builds.toml(5)\f[B]\f[R] for details.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[CR]<package>\f[R]
The package(s) to build.
Possible values are all keys under the \f[CR]build\f[R] attribute in the
environment\(cqs \f[CR]manifest.toml\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-stability <stability>\f[R]
Perform a nix expression build using a base package set of the given
stability as tracked by the catalog server.
A stability (e.g., \f[CR]\(dqstable\(dq\f[R]) identifies a curated
nixpkgs revision managed by the catalog server.
When omitted, the base package set is derived from the environment\(cqs
\f[CR]toplevel\f[R] group; if no \f[CR]toplevel\f[R] group exists, the
\f[CR]\(dqstable\(dq\f[R] stability is used by default.
An explicit \f[CR]\-\-stability\f[R] value overrides both of these
defaults.
Cannot be used with manifest builds.
.SS Environment Options
If no environment is specified for an environment command, the
environment in the current directory or the active environment that was
last activated is used.
.TP
\f[CR]\-d\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-dir\f[R]
Path containing a .flox/ directory.
.SS General Options
.TP
\f[CR]\-h\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]
Prints help information.
.PP
The following options can be passed when running any \f[CR]flox\f[R]
subcommand but must be specified \f[I]before\f[R] the subcommand.
.TP
\f[CR]\-v\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R]
Increase logging verbosity.
Invoke multiple times for increasing detail.
.TP
\f[CR]\-q\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-quiet\f[R]
Silence logs except for errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Building a simple pure package
.IP "1." 3
Add build instructions to the manifest:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# file: .flox/env/manifest.toml\f[R]

\&...
\f[B][build]\f[R]
hello.command = \(aq\(aq\(aq
# produce something and move it to $out
mkdir \-p $out
echo \(dqhello world\(dq >> $out/hello.txt
\(aq\(aq\(aq
description = \(dqProduces a file containing \(aqhello world\(aq\(dq
version = \(dq0.0.0\(dq
.EE
.IP "2." 3
Build the package and verify its contents:
.IP
.EX
$ flox build hello
$ ls ./result\-hello
hello.txt
$ cat ./result\-hello/hello.txt
hello world
.EE
.SS Building a simple multi\-stage app
Assume a simple \f[CR]nodejs\f[R] project
.IP
.EX
\&.
├── .git/
├── package\-lock.json
├── package.json
├── public/
├── README.md
├── src/
\&...
.EE
.IP "1." 3
Initialize a Flox environment
.IP
.EX
flox init
.EE
.IP "2." 3
Install dependencies and add build instructions
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# file: .flox/env/manifest.toml\f[R]
version = 1

\f[B][install]\f[R]
nodejs.pkg\-path = \(dqnodejs\(dq
rsync.pkg\-path = \(dqrsync\(dq

\f[I]# install node dependencies using npm\f[R]
\f[I]# disable the sandbox to allow access to the network\f[R]
\f[B][build]\f[R]
deps.command = \(aq\(aq\(aq
npm ci
mkdir \-p $out
mv node_modules $out/node_modules
\(aq\(aq\(aq
deps.sandbox = \(dqoff\(dq

\f[I]# build the application using previously fetched dependencies\f[R]
app.command = \(aq\(aq\(aq
rsync \-lr ${deps}/node_modules ./
npm run build
mv dist $out/
\(aq\(aq\(aq
.EE
.IP "3." 3
Verify the result
.IP
.EX
npx serve result\-app
.EE
